hw/sh4: ze0 HPB-DMAC serial channels: transmit to the SCIF, per-channel interrupts (TODO M8.C2a).

After the launcher checks pass (M8.C1c) the audio-network protocol of the
EU2011 applications (CInaiProtocol) loops on "write failed".  Its driver,
serial_scif.dll, sends through HPB-DMAC channel 10 and receives through
channels 1/3/7 (register trace, hpbdmac-trace=on):

  ch10  DSAR 0xa999a000 (RAM)  DDAR 0xffe4100c (SCIF1 FTDR)  DTCR 8
        DPTR 0x0001  DCR 0x00000020
  ch1/3/7  DSAR 0xffe4n014 (SCIFn FRDR)  DDAR/DDAR1 two 0x80-byte RAM
        planes (0xa999a800/880, 0xa999c800/880, 0xa999e800/880)
        DTCR 0x80  DPTR 0x0n00  DCR 0x00052000

nk.exe's interrupt rows for these channels (INTEVT 0xb60 + 0x20 * ch:
0xb80 fn 0x8803e654, 0xbc0 fn 0x8803e6cc, 0xc40 fn 0x8803e7bc, 0xca0
fn 0x8803e8a8; IntrPrio 14) mask INT2 bit 19, clear the channel bit of
DINTMR0 (+0x814) and return SYSINTR 0x13.

ze0_hpbdmac.c: one interrupt output per channel (sysbus irq n = channel
n).  With the new property serial (machine option hpbdmac-serial, default
on) an armed channel whose DDAR is a SCIF FTDR and whose DSAR is memory
writes its DTCR bytes one at a time to that FTDR (the sh_serial stub
behind it) and completes: DTCR 0, DSTSR.0 clear, DINTSR0 bit set,
interrupt while DINTMR0 has the bit.  A channel whose DSAR is a SCIF
FRDR stays armed: nothing models the peer, so no data arrives.
hpbdmac-trace logs each transmit with its bytes.

ze0_intc.c: sources HPBDMAC1/3/7/10 with the INTEVTs above on INT2
bit 19 (level 14 as the table's IntrPrio); INT2 +0x3c bit 19 is the OR
of all six DMAC sources.  ze0.c wires channels 1, 3, 7, 8, 9, 10.
